Found during k128, Windows only and unverified. The editor runs in a Job Object with KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE (the no-zombie guarantee). A CrashReportClient the editor spawns after a crash is in that job too, so closing the job when the editor exits may kill the reporter at once, before it has written or sent its report and before the restart delay. The restart-time killCrashReporter then only ever hits reporters outside the job.

Needs a Windows run: crash the editor (e.g. via a debug command), watch whether CrashReportClient survives the editor exit, and whether Saved/Crashes gets a complete report. If it is killed, the fix must keep KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE for the launcher shutting down (no zombies) — e.g. a breakaway for the crash reporter only, or holding the job open until the reporter ends — never by dropping the job assignment.

Research input 2026-11-21: docs/research/2026-11-21-windows-processes.md sections 2 and 3 (job close, breakaway for one helper) and docs/research/2026-11-21-unreal-editor.md section 2 (crash handling). Unchecked. Experiments W3, W4 and U4 are the Windows run this card asks for.

From k198 (2026-11-21): Epic's API page for SetOutOfProcessCrashReporterPid says CrashReportClientEditor can be configured to wait for a crash, so it may run for the editor's whole life rather than appear after a crash. editorPreStart treats it as a leftover; inference: taskkill /IM on a start that follows no crash would also kill the crash monitor of another healthy editor on the machine. Check in the same Windows run (U4).

Windows run 2026-11-22 (part of W3/W4/U4), found while running k208. Windows 11 Pro 26H2 build 26300.9711; UE 5.7.4 (CL 51494982) Launcher, also 5.8.4 where noted; disposable Blueprint project with the UE LLM Toolkit; unreagent d4edd23 (Go 1.27.0, built on the machine), unreagent.yaml with agent.enabled false, restart: never. Raw: C:\Temp\unreagent-probe\runs\12..15, 30..34 on that machine.

1. The reporter lives for the editor's whole life. ~1 s after the editor starts it logs "Started CrashReportClient (pid=N)"; the running process is CrashReportClientEditor.exe "-READ=620 -WRITE=632 -MONITOR=<editor pid> -ProcessGroupId=... -unattended -ConcertServer=... -RespawnedInstance" (5.7.4; -unattended only when the editor has it). Its parent is the first instance N, not the editor: the instance the editor starts respawns itself. A parent-PID walk from the editor never finds it. 5.8.4: same -MONITOR form.

2. Crash under unreagent CUTS THE REPORT SHORT (CONFIRMS the card). Console "debug crash" in the editor, run 33: editor exit 3 after 3.4 s ("The editor failed and is not restarted"); the respawned reporter was alive at that moment and gone 1.5 s later. Saved/Crashes/UECC-*/ then holds CrashContext.runtime-xml (111897 bytes, well-formed), CrashReportClient.ini, the log copy and UEMinidump.dmp (1.79 MB), and it never changed again. Same crash started directly (run 34, -unattended, no job): the XML is 113184 bytes at the editor's exit and 191107 bytes 5 s later; the reporter fills it in after the editor is gone, then exits by itself. Field diff unreagent vs direct: CallStack empty vs 627 bytes (symbolicated: UnrealEditor_Engine, _Core, _UnrealEd ...), Modules empty vs 77 KB, Threads 72 vs 73 KB; ErrorMessage ("Fatal error: [File:...UnrealEngine.cpp] [Line: 11310] Crashing the gamethread at your request"), CrashType (Assert), PCallStack (raw module+offset) and the minidump are present in both. So under unreagent the agent gets the error text and raw addresses but no symbolicated call stack.

3. Job close with a healthy editor (runs 30/31: console "stop ue", and unreagent itself TerminateProcess-ed): editor exit 0 within 0.44 s; reporter (respawned instance included), zenserver, crashpad_handler and EpicWebHelper all gone within 2 s, so the respawn does not break away from the job.

4. Without a job, killing only the editor (taskkill /F) or a crash without -unattended: the reporter survives; after "debug crash" without -unattended its window "Probe57 Crash Reporter" stayed open waiting for a human, still there 3+ min later.

5. killCrashReporter: because the reporter is a lifetime monitor, the taskkill /F /IM CrashReportClientEditor.exe before a start that follows no crash ends the crash monitor of every other editor on the machine (k198 inference, now backed by the process facts; not run against a second healthy editor).

For the owner (unreagent-worker): keep KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE; the run suggests holding the job open after a crash exit until the reporter exits (it took ~5 s here), or a breakaway for the reporter only. And target the reporter by its -MONITOR=<pid> argument instead of /IM.

Scout read of the UE 5.8.4 engine source on 2026-11-22 (source only, nothing run; paths relative to the engine root). Engine side confirmed: the editor starts CrashReportClientEditor as a monitor in its static init, for its whole life (Runtime/Core/Private/Windows/WindowsPlatformCrashContext.cpp 54-55, 1390-1393), detached but without CREATE_BREAKAWAY_FROM_JOB (WindowsPlatformProcess.cpp 567-593), so it sits in our Job Object. On a crash the reporter acks the editor after minidump and stacks are written, then still writes CrashContext.runtime-xml and does the unattended send (Programs/CrashReportClient/Private/CrashReportClientApp.cpp 691-720, 1329-1340); the editor exits right after the ack. A job close at editor exit can therefore cut the report short. Whether it does needs the run (experiment U4).

Point 5 measured (2026-11-22, run 52, same machine; UE 5.8.4, unreagent d4edd23 -no-agent, both editors started by the probe): editor A started directly on a disposable Blueprint project (-unattended), its monitor CrashReportClientEditor -MONITOR=<A's pid> running. Then unreagent started editor B on another disposable project, a plain first start (no crash before it). Within 3.7 s of unreagent's start A's monitor was gone, while editor A kept running; 35 s later A still had no monitor (the editor does not restart it), and B had its own new monitor (-MONITOR=<B's pid>). So the taskkill /F /IM CrashReportClientEditor.exe in editorPreStart does end the crash monitor of every other editor on the machine: a later crash of such an editor goes unreported (no reporter left to write Saved/Crashes or send it). Side fact: the editor logs "Started CrashReportClient (pid=X)" and the process with -MONITOR is a child of X, not of the editor, so a fix that targets "our" reporter should match the -MONITOR=<editor pid> argument (or the job membership), not the parent pid.

