unreagent-worker 2026-11-23, decisions (mine) and supervisor state per branch:

1. Grace only after a FAILED exit (err != nil) of a service with ServiceSpec.ReporterGrace > 0, set only for 'ue' (agent untouched). Branch: runService exit case (<-waitCh): the run has ended on its own, cmd/job not yet cleared — the same branch that closed the job before. There the loop looks into the job once, synchronously (reporterIn); only if a CrashReportClient(Editor).exe is in the job's own process list (QueryInformationJobObject/BasicProcessIdList + QueryFullProcessImageNameW) does the job go to the closeAfterReporter goroutine; otherwise it is closed at once as before. Exit 0, stop(), ctrlRestart's stop and shutdown close at once unchanged. Why: a clean exit 0 has no report to finish; a stop/restart is a kill, no crash report.
2. Grace 30 s (const crashReporterGrace, cmd/launcher/lifecycle.go): run 34 needed ~5 s; 6x margin for cold symbols / slow send; a reporter waiting on its window (no -unattended) is ended at 30 s. No config option: nothing measured argues for tuning.
3. Next start waits for the kept job (start(), before PreStart, after the shutdown check). State: service not running (cmd == nil), lingerDone set only if a reporter was seen. Restart policy: the backoff timer ran since the exit, so the start comes at max(restartDelay, reporter done), not the sum. Why wait at all: a new editor next to the old job could take up what that job still holds (zenserver - inference, not measured) and lose it at the close; also cleanRecovery cannot empty Saved/Crashes under a writing reporter. During the wait preStarting is set, so status/timeouts say busy pre-start (pinned by test). Trade-off: a hung reporter blocks the loop up to 30 s minus the restart delay; a control call in that window gets the existing 'busy with its pre-start step' timeout.
4. Shutdown (ctx done): closeAfterReporter selects on ctx and closes the job at once; runService's ctx branch waits for that close before returning (pinned: returns <2 s with a 1 h grace). Console close/crash of the launcher: the OS closes the handle, KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE does the rest. Job creation/assignment and KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE unchanged; no breakaway.
5. Pre-start kill removed entirely (killCrashReporter/crashReporterKillers gone, also the Linux pkill). Every reporter of ours is in our job and dies with it (≤30 s after a crash, at once otherwise); the only thing the kill still reached is a reporter of an editor we did not start — not ours. No -MONITOR targeting kept: it would only kill our own reporter, i.e. exactly what the grace protects. unreal.killCrashReporter stays accepted (KnownFields would otherwise break existing configs), defaults nil, run() logs a WARN when set.

